A receiver method belongs to a struct or choice declared in the same source
file. Define it outside the type declaration, alongside the other top-level
functions. Its first parameter names the value that receives the call.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Vector2 = | x F64, y F64 |

    length_squared |this Vector2| -> F64 {
        return this.x * this.x + this.y * this.y
    }

    vector = Vector2(3.0, 4.0)
    length = vector.length_squared()
]

The dot supplies `vector` as the receiver. The parameter `this` gives the
body shared access to its fields. To change those fields, declare a
@#mutable-receivers (mutable receiver).

### Receiver declaration

Reserve `this` for the first parameter and its uses inside the method.
A method takes exactly one receiver and callers use dot syntax. Any other
parameters take arguments inside the call's parentheses. A method can also
declare a @../functions/#returns-and-multiple-values (Never return contract)
with `-> !`.

For a generic struct or choice, the method's type parameters must line up
with the type's declaration. Use a free function to work with a builtin
scalar, an imported type, a dependency's type, an external opaque type or a
type declared in another file. Collections and builders have their own
builtin operations.

### Visibility

A file that receives a value can call the methods its type provides, even
when that file doesn't bind the type name. To name the type in an annotation,
constructor, conformance declaration or bound, bring the type name into scope.

A module that exposes a type also exposes its same-file methods. Methods
can't be imported, aliased or re-exported independently, and a namespace
binding for a type doesn't make its methods namespace fields. A field and
a method on the same type need different names.

Private methods can propagate implicit failure within their module. An
exported method without an error slot handles that failure locally. See
@../errors (Errors).

### Constants and receiver methods

Folding a struct instance into a constant produces a data-only const record.
Runtime code can read its folded fields but can't call runtime methods on the
record. See @../constants/#const-records (Const records).

For an eligible same-file struct or choice, a const method takes a shared
receiver and carries the same `#` marker as a const function. The receiver and
arguments must all be available at compile time. Moth evaluates the receiver
once, before the explicit arguments, and checks its shared access in the same
way as a const parameter. The method can return one or more success values.
This contract excludes mutable or generic const methods and methods on anonymous
const records. See @../functions/#function-declarations (Function declarations)
and @../progress (Progress) for current compiler support.
